   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/10546695/#comment15332318_10546830",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2012-07-20T20:45:36Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "@curiousguy: If the code has a defined behaviour then, by definition, it can&#39;t be relying on such destructors being called. I&#39;m not sure that this part of the standard says very much other than circularly give a hint at the definition of &quot;rely on&quot; but I don&#39;t think that it&#39;s inconsistent."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 15334997,
   "parent_id": 10546830,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": 963864,
   "author_display_name": "curiousguy",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/10546695/#comment15334997_10546830",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2012-07-20T23:42:45Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "&quot;<i>I don&#39;t think that it&#39;s inconsistent</i>&quot; No. It&#39;s just tautological. You could as well say that code that expects <code>cout &lt;&lt; &quot;&quot;;</code> to print <code>Hello, World</code> is undefined... the std has plenty of absurd statements, and this is one my favourites."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 15335185,
   "parent_id": 10546830,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": 845092,
   "author_display_name": "Mooing Duck",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/10546695/#comment15335185_10546830",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2012-07-21T00:04:39Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "@curiousguy: Undefined behavior merely means that the C++ standard makes no claims as to what your program will or will not do, and any bad things that happen are your fault.  As such, it should be avoided.  Yes, something happens, but the C++ standard can&#39;t guess what it would be."
  },
